<h2>The question</h2>
<p>
  The operator's complaint: reviewers do a lot of verification work that should have been the creator's to
  discharge — sometimes going <em>"very far"</em> to check a claim the creator could have proven cheaply at
  write time. Should a reviewer be able to answer <strong>"unverifiable as submitted"</strong> — distinct from
  accept/changes, a refusal to do the creator's proof work rather than a verdict on correctness — and should
  that refusal be captured as a structured, attributable creator-side gap that harvests forward into dispatch
  briefs, prep checklists, and gates? This topic grounds the decision
  <a href="/backlog/xsuqas6-a-reviewer-may-answer-unverifiable-as-submitted-a-creator-pr/">a reviewer may answer
  "unverifiable as submitted"</a>.
</p>

<h2>Six bodies of prior art, reported at the strength the evidence supports</h2>
<p>
  This survey follows the same rule its own subject argues for: report what a source <em>actually says</em>,
  named and specific, not what its reputation suggests — and say plainly where the fit is loose rather than
  stretch a citation to cover a gap.
</p>

