      <td><strong>Design by Contract</strong> (Bertrand Meyer, <em>Object-Oriented Software Construction</em>)</td>
      <td>The client (caller) must satisfy preconditions before calling; the supplier (callee) must satisfy
        postconditions <em>given the precondition holds</em>, and is explicitly not obligated to re-check it.
        Paraphrased consistently across independent secondary sources: "either the condition is part of the
        precondition and must be guaranteed by the client, or it is not stated in the precondition and must be
        handled by the supplier … never in both" — an absolute rule against defensive double-checking.</td>
      <td><strong>Strong, structural.</strong> The sharpest precedent for a division of proof labor where one
        party is entitled not to re-derive what the other owed. Gap: about <em>runtime</em> obligation, not
        review-time evidence — the shape of the argument transfers, not a literal review-process precedent. A
        clean page-cited verbatim quote could not be pulled from Meyer's scanned source PDFs in this pass;
        treat the paraphrase as high-confidence, not verbatim.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>TDD / red-green</strong> (Kent Beck, <em>Test-Driven Development: By Example</em>)</td>
      <td>Two rules, widely and consistently quoted: "write new code only if an automated test has failed" and
        "eliminate duplication."</td>
      <td><strong>Strong fit for machine-checkable evidence specifically.</strong> The failing-then-passing test
        is the creator's proof artifact — produced before/alongside the claim, checkable by a third party
        without hand-verification. Beck's own "done-ness" framing in exactly that language was not located;
        the restatement above is reasonable but not a confirmed quote.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Falsifiability</strong> (Karl Popper) and <strong>Dijkstra</strong></td>
      <td>Dijkstra, confirmed via primary-source transcription — <strong>EWD249</strong> ("Notes on Structured
        Programming," 1970), "On The Reliability of Mechanisms": <em>"Program testing can be used to show the
        presence of bugs, but never to show their absence!"</em> A related phrasing appears in "The Humble
        Programmer" (1972 Turing lecture, <em>CACM</em> 15(10)). Popper's falsifiability is connected to
        software testing via Kaner, Bach &amp; Pettichord's <em>Lessons Learned in Software Testing</em>
        (2001), corroborated across secondary sources but not pinned to a verbatim page.</td>
      <td><strong>Dijkstra: confirmed and citable.</strong> <strong>Popper-to-review: not found.</strong> No
        literature was located that explicitly frames code <em>review</em> (as opposed to testing) in
        Popperian terms — "a reviewer's job is to falsify, not verify exhaustively" is this session's
        synthesis of the testing literature, not a documented claim about review. Flagged rather than
        oversold.</td>
    </tr>
