      <td><strong>Author-supplied-test-as-gate policy</strong> (Google eng-practices)</td>
      <td><code>google.github.io/eng-practices</code>: <em>"we expect developers to test CLs well-enough that
        they work correctly by the time they get to code review."</em></td>
      <td><strong>Moderate.</strong> Puts initial proof burden on the author, as a real, named, public policy.
        No explicit language was found stating a CL may be rejected <em>solely</em> for missing tests, nor a
        documented reviewer right to decline re-derivation — this decision's specific verdict has no direct
        precedent here. Bacchelli &amp; Bird's ICSE 2013 modern-code-review paper (Microsoft) was confirmed to
        exist and scope, but its findings text was not retrievable in this pass — not cited as direct
        support.</td>
    </tr>
