<h2>A concrete, everyday occurrence of the same third answer</h2>
<p>
  Outside the academic literature, the pattern this decision proposes is already ubiquitous in bug trackers:
  a maintainer's <strong>"needs more info" / "can't reproduce"</strong> resolution is a well-known third answer
  distinct from "confirmed bug" and "works as intended" — a refusal to keep investigating until the reporter
  supplies reproduction steps, not a verdict on whether the bug is real. GitHub's <code>needs-repro</code> /
  <code>needs-info</code> label convention and Bugzilla's <code>INCOMPLETE</code> resolution are the same
  move this decision proposes for code review: the burden of making the claim checkable returns to whoever
  made it.
</p>

<h2>What this decision does with the survey</h2>
<p>
  Four of six areas (Design by Contract, TDD, Dijkstra's half of falsifiability, and Google's author-test
  policy) give solid, citable support for the general shape: the party making a claim is the cheaper, more
  natural place for its proof to originate, and machine-checkable evidence (a test, a resolved citation, a
  quoted source) is what actually discharges that obligation — a self-declared assurance is not. Two areas
  (Tricorder, and the Popper-to-review connection) are real but do not say exactly what a naive citation
  would want, and are reported at that honest strength rather than stretched — the same discipline this
  program has already ratified against overclaiming
  (<a href="/backlog/3362-state-a-check-s-predicate-and-candidate-set-never-assert-com/">#3362</a>). Full
  detail in the linked session report.
</p>
